      '@type': 'Question',
      name: "What is Rousseau's Confessions about?",
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Confessions (published 1782–1789) by Jean-Jacques Rousseau is one of the most important autobiographies ever written — the work that invented modern autobiography and the candid literary self. Rousseau claims to show 'a man in all the truth of nature' and gives a remarkably frank account of his life from his Calvinist childhood in Geneva through his years as a wandering musician and tutor, his love affairs, his philosophical awakening in 1749, and his eventual fame. The Confessions did not hesitate to include shameful material: the theft of a ribbon blamed on an innocent servant, his abandonment of five children to a Paris foundling hospital, his masochistic desires. The combination of philosophic seriousness and shameless candor was unprecedented. Our full guide covers 8 picks for readers of Rousseau's revolutionary autobiography." },
